"text": "ICMLDE 2026 · CBIS-DDSM · INbreast One shortcut, two failures , two fixes. A mammography model can learn to guess cancer from how dense the breast is instead of from the lesion. This project shows that the shortcut damages the model in two separate ways, and that each one needs its own fix. A known density–cancer link was added to the training data only, five training methods were compared over 13 runs, and every model was checked for both problems. Each method plotted on the two problems Horizontal axis: worst-group AUROC gap. Vertical axis: share of the heat map outside the breast (SAS). Baseline 0.357 and 0.557; GRL 0.664 and 0.619; distance correlation 0.083 and 0.650; CAM regularization 0.331 and 0.018; both fixes 0.065 and 0.021. 0.0 0.0 0.1 0.1 0.2 0.2 0.3 0.3 0.4 0.4 0.5 0.5 0.6 0.6 0.7 0.7 Group gap in AUROC: feature problem → Off-breast attention (SAS): attention problem → Baseline GRL Distance correlation CAM regularization Both fixes Point at a method to see its scores. Lower is better on both axes. CBIS-DDSM, averages over 13 runs. Each fix moves along its own axis; only the combination reaches the shaded corner, where both problems are fixed. 0.357 → 0.083 Group gap, baseline to distance correlation 0.557 → 0.018 Off-breast attention, baseline to CAM regularization 0.065 · 0.021 Gap and off-breast attention with both fixes 0.705 → 0.684 AUROC cost of both fixes, about two points Two problems, two fixes # Feature problem Density hides in the features What goes wrong The model keeps density inside its learned features and uses it to predict cancer, so it fails on patients who break the pattern. Measured by Group AUROC gap and a density probe Fix A distance-correlation penalty between features and density Attention problem The heat map leaves the breast What goes wrong Grad-CAM heat maps light up the background and image edges instead of breast tissue. Measured by SAS: the share of the heat map outside the breast Fix A penalty on any attention outside the breast mask Finding: each fix solves only its own problem. Used together, they solve both, and this holds with weaker shortcuts, other backbones, another density definition, many penalty weights and digital mammograms. Results at a glance # CBIS-DDSM, average ± SD over 13 runs. Lower is better for the gap, SAS and the density probe. Method AUROC Group gap SAS Density probe Baseline 0.705 ± 0.018 0.357 ± 0.087 0.557 ± 0.046 0.905 ± 0.012 Gradient reversal (GRL) 0.549 ± 0.042 0.664 ± 0.203 0.619 ± 0.049 0.905 ± 0.010 Distance correlation 0.696 ± 0.023 0.083 ± 0.090 0.650 ± 0.053 0.862 ± 0.014 CAM regularization 0.689 ± 0.027 0.331 ± 0.066 0.018 ± 0.004 0.906 ± 0.012 Both fixes 0.684 ± 0.023 0.065 ± 0.078 0.021 ± 0.003 0.861 ± 0.014 Image placeholder: study pipeline Add docs/assets/figures/architecture.png How the study works: the training data is given a density shortcut, five training methods are compared, and each model is checked for both problems. Explore # Research The question, the datasets, how the shortcut is added and the five methods. Results Main comparison, sweeps, robustness, published methods and calibration. Code Install, get the data and run every experiment yourself. About The people behind the work, the paper, a glossary and references."
