"text": "Research overview The question # If a mammography model leans on breast density to predict cancer, can one change to training fix it? Short answer: no. The shortcut causes two different problems, and fixing one leaves the other in place. What this work adds # Two problems, two fixes. Removing density from the features fixes the group gap but not the heat maps. Penalizing off-breast attention fixes the heat maps but not the gap. This holds across many settings and a second dataset. The combined fix works. Using both penalties together fixes both problems for a small loss in accuracy. Its effect on calibration and on the best decision threshold is also measured. Adversarial training is fragile. A common adversarial method fails badly with default settings but works well once tuned. Even then, no adversarial, mutual-information or group-reweighting method tested here fixes the heat maps. Pages in this section # Page Topic Background Why breast density causes trouble for AI models Related work What earlier studies tried and what they left open Datasets The two datasets, how density is defined, and how the shortcut is added Method The network and the five training methods Evaluation The measures and statistics used to compare methods"
