"text": "Publication Paper # Decoupling Shortcut Learning Axes in Mammography Classification Dhiraaj K V and Raghesh Krishnan K International Conference on Machine Learning and Data Engineering (ICMLDE 2026), to appear in Procedia Computer Science (Elsevier). Topics: shortcut learning · mammography · breast density · explainable AI · worst-group robustness · distance correlation Summary # AI models that look for cancer in mammograms can learn to rely on breast density instead of the lesion. This work splits that shortcut into two parts: density hidden in the model's features, and attention that drifts outside the breast. A density–cancer link (r ≈ 0.6) is added to the CBIS-DDSM training data only, and four fixes are compared with a baseline over 13 random seeds. Removing density from the features with distance correlation shrinks the group AUROC gap from 0.357 to 0.083, but the attention stays outside the breast. Penalizing off-breast attention with CAM regularization cuts it from 0.557 to 0.018, but the gap stays almost the same. Both together fix both problems (gap 0.065, off-breast attention 0.021) for about two AUROC points. This split shows up again for a weaker shortcut, for DenseNet-121 and ResNet-50, for the clinical density grouping, over many penalty weights, and on 192 INbreast digital mammograms. A carefully tuned adversary can also narrow the gap, but none of the feature or reweighting methods repairs the attention. Each problem needs its own fix. Project progress # Stage Status Experiments on CBIS-DDSM (13 seeds, five methods) Done Second dataset: INbreast Done Robustness, sweep, calibration and mask studies Done ICMLDE 2026 submission, revision and reply to reviewers Revised paper submitted on 31 October 2026 Camera-ready paper November 2026 M.Tech dissertation (24CS798, Dissertation Phase) In progress"
